WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.300
- [Narrator] Imagine that
all the Earth's oceans

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:03.890
disappeared for a day.

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:05.200
And imagine that you,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.440
being the excellent explorer you are,

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.730
decided to go investigate.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.350
You'd travel across the continental shelf,

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:16.110
down the continental slope,
and across the abyssal plain.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.350
You'd see gaping trenches
miles deep in the ground,

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.620
and mountains taller than any
mountain on the continents.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.150
And you might wonder,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.090
what causes such dramatic
landscapes to be formed?

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.960
Earth's lithosphere, which
is made up of the crust,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.330
and the upper part of the mantle,

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.170
is broken up into large
puzzle piece-like chunks

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:36.920
called tectonic plates.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.240
These tectonic plates move around slowly

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.800
over millions of years

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.100
on the section of the mantle
below the lithosphere,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.973
which we call the asthenosphere.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.250
There are two types of tectonic plates,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.940
oceanic plates and continental plates.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.890
The continental plates,
as you probably guessed,

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.870
are the ones that make up the continents.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.400
The oceanic plates are the ones
that make up the sea floor.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.520
The main difference between oceanic plates

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:02.910
and continental plates

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.470
is the type of crust found on each plate.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.500
Oceanic crust and continental crust

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.410
are made out of different kinds of rock.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.150
The continental crust
contains a lot of granite,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.020
which is an igneous rock.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.660
This means that it was made out of rock

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.090
that was once molten.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.550
The oceanic crust has
a lot of basalt in it,

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.840
which is another kind of igneous rock.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.380
The difference in the kinds of rock

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.900
that the crusts are made out of

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.610
means that the oceanic crust

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.870
is denser than the continental crust.

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:30.340
If you took a cubic centimeter

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.100
of the rock from the continental crust,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.590
it would be about 2.7 grams.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.830
A cubic centimeter from the oceanic crust,

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.910
on the other hand, would
weigh about three grams.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:43.140
While this difference in density
might not seem like much,

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.430
it completely changes how
tectonic plates interact.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.360
The high density of oceanic crust

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.220
causes oceanic plates to
sink into the asthenosphere

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.620
a bit more than continental plates do.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.880
When an oceanic plate
collides with another plate

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.550
at a convergent boundary,
it always follows this rule.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.820
The denser plates always dives
beneath the less dense plate.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.210
When it's an oceanic plate

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.370
and a continental plate converging,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.770
the denser oceanic plate
is the one that dips down.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.630
And when it's two oceanic
plates that are colliding,

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.520
the older denser oceanic plate

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.480
will move under the newer
and less dense oceanic plate.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.350
And over time, the denser plate

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.660
will be recycled into the asthenosphere.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.110
The place where the plates collide

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.810
is called a subduction zone.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.310
This bending of the denser
plates under the other

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.930
creates a trench.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:33.430
The deepest one is the Mariana's Trench,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.580
which is located where the Pacific plates

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.600
dives under the Mariana plates.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.950
The trench is almost seven miles deep.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.110
The plate that sinks
into the asthenosphere

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.760
often has some water and
fluids trapped inside of it.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.810
These fluids heat up, and
bubble to the surface.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.030
The hot fluids can cause
sections of mantle rock

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.810
to melt into magma,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.350
which then rises to the
surface, and creates volcanoes.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:57.330
And you might be wondering,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.030
if the sea floor is
constantly being destroyed,

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.270
then what keeps the Earth from shrinking?

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.730
Well, new sea floor is
constantly being created too.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.740
This happens when two tectonic plates

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.560
move away from each other
at a divergent boundary.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.240
When two plates diverge
in the middle of an ocean,

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.530
it creates a kind of
underwater mountain range,

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.750
called a mid-ocean ridge.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.900
As the plates move apart
at mid-ocean ridges,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.900
magma moves up, cools, and
forms a new younger lithosphere.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.400
You can think of this process

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.700
like a really slow conveyor belt.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.940
New ocean sea floor is made at the ridges,

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.890
and moves away from them
over millions of years.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.080
This means that the oceanic crust

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.760
that is closest to the
ridge is the youngest.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.280
And as it moves away from the ridge,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.510
the crust gets older and older.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.760
As it ages, the crust becomes
cooler, and more dense.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.963
And eventually, it dips back down

00:03:46.963 --> 00:03:49.860
into the asthenosphere at the trenches.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.470
The sea floor is perhaps

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.680
the most unexplored part of our planet.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.650
So if they do drain the ocean,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.770
and you go for a walk at
the bottom of the sea,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.370
I hope you'll tell us all
what it's like down there.

